---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import DynamoRing from '../../components/DynamoRing.astro';
import { ring, scenarios, short } from '../../lib/dynamo';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
---

<Base
	title="Simple Dynamo: a replicated key-value store"
	description="An interactive ring: how my 2018–19 Dynamo-style coursework store places keys, replicates writes, recovers a failed node and fails over a read."
>
	<article>
		<p class="eyebrow mono">University at Buffalo coursework, 2018–19</p>
		<h1>Simple Dynamo: a replicated key-value store</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			A Dynamo-style distributed key-value store running on five Android emulator nodes, written in Java. Keys and node IDs
			are hashed with SHA-1 onto a consistent-hashing ring. Each key belongs to the first node clockwise and is replicated to
			that node's next two successors, so every key has three copies. Nodes talk over raw TCP sockets, with a background
			server thread and serialized client tasks. Each replica stores data in SQLite, and a newer write replaces an older one.
			Reads go to the key's coordinator and fall back to the last replica in the chain if the coordinator is down. Writes that
			a dead replica misses are buffered by the sender, and a restarting node pulls them back from its peers when it rejoins.
		</p>

		<h2>The ring</h2>
		<p>
			Each emulator is named by its port. Sorting the SHA-1 hashes of the five port numbers gives the ring order, the same
			order the code uses:
		</p>
		<ol class="order mono">
			{
				ring.map((n) => (
					<li>
						{n.port} <span>{short(n.hash)}…</span>
					</li>
				))
			}
		</ol>
		<p>Step through each scenario with Prev and Next, or the arrow keys.</p>

		{scenarios.map((s, i) => <DynamoRing scenario={s} fig={i + 1} />)}

		<h2>What it doesn't do</h2>
		<ul class="limits">
			<li>No quorum: a write is sent to all three replicas in turn, and a read trusts the one replica that answers.</li>
			<li>No versioning or vector clocks. Stubs for them exist in the code, but nothing calls them.</li>
			<li>The buffer of failed writes is one list per sender, not one per destination.</li>
		</ul>

		<p class="code mono"><a href="https://github.com/Charan2396/Amazon-Dynamo-DB-">Code on GitHub →</a></p>
		<p class="back mono"><a href={url('projects/')}>← All projects</a></p>
	</article>
</Base>

<style>
	.eyebrow {
		margin: 0;
		color: var(--line);
		font-size: 0.8125rem;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
	}
	h1 {
		max-width: var(--measure);
		margin-block: 0.5rem 1rem;
		font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 5vw, 2.75rem);
	}
	.lede,
	p,
	.limits {
		max-width: var(--measure);
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.1875rem;
	}
	h2 {
		margin-block: 2.5rem 0.75rem;
		font-size: 1.375rem;
	}
	.order {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
		padding: 0;
		list-style: none;
		font-size: 0.875rem;
	}
	.order li {
		padding: 0.25rem 0.5rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--line);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: var(--surface);
	}
	.order span {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.limits {
		padding-left: 1.25rem;
	}
	.code {
		margin-top: 2rem;
		font-size: 0.875rem;
	}
	.back {
		margin-top: 1rem;
		font-size: 0.875rem;
	}
</style>
